      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Acute coronary involvement (ACI) due to acute aortic dissection (AAD) type A is potentially fatal. We examined selected patients with AAD type A, which had evolved over 14 years, and acute coronary involvement. The purpose of this study was to determine the characteristics of patients with ACI due to AAD type A."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Between 1997 and 2011, we recruited 20 patients (14.1%) with ACI (14 men, 6 women; mean age: 51.8 ± 11.8 years; age range: 35-79 years) from 142 patients who had undergone surgical repair of AAD type A."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "We propose a novel 4-category classification scheme based on the surgical pathological findings. The right coronary artery was involved in 15 patients, and the left was involved in 5 patients. Fourteen patients had preoperative myocardial ischemia. In the other 6 patients, acute coronary involvement was found intraoperatively. Patients with ACI were significantly younger than those without ACI (51.8 ± 11.8 vs. 61.0 ± 11.8; p = 0.001), a lower prevalence of intramural hematoma (5.0% vs. 32.8%; p = 0.011), a higher aortic regurgitation rate (95.0% vs. 53.5%; p = 0.001). Patients presenting with ACI had an in-hospital mortality rate of 20.0% (4/20), while those without ACI had an in-hospital mortality rate of 19.7% (24/122)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Acute coronary involvement due to AAD type A is not always associated with coronary malperfusion. Patients with ACI were much younger, had a higher aortic regurgitation rate, and, less commonly, had intramural hematoma. This new classification scheme would make it more convenient for surgeons to decide on treatment options for this special cohort."
          }
        ]
      },
